@node Intro "Introduction"

@{b}Introduction@{ub}

NextSync keeps folders on your Amiga in step with folders on a Nextcloud
server. It talks WebDAV over HTTPS with real certificate checking, so it
works with any ordinary Nextcloud installation, and it needs nothing on
the server side.

It is a two way sync. Something you change on the Amiga goes up, something
that changed on the server comes down, and it works out which is which by
remembering what both sides looked like after the last run. Folders are
followed all the way down, not just their top level.

It is one binary of about 49K with a Workbench interface and a Shell
interface in it, and it uses only what is already in your Kickstart ROM to
draw itself, so there is no GUI toolkit to install.

Written for the A1200 and A4000, tested on a 68020 without an FPU and on a
68040.

@{" Contents " link Main}

@endnode


@node Require "Requirements"

@{b}Requirements@{ub}

  @{b}AmigaOS 3.1 or later@{ub}
      3.1 is the floor. Newer is fine.

  @{b}A 68020 or better@{ub}
      Not NextSync's doing: AmiSSL v5 is built for 68020 and up.

  @{b}About 6 MB of free RAM@{ub}
      Most of it is OpenSSL. Check with Avail before you wonder why a
      sync failed.

  @{b}A TCP/IP stack@{ub}
      Roadshow, AmiTCP, Miami, or anything else that provides
      bsdsocket.library. NextSync does not care which.

  @{b}AmiSSL 5.x@{ub}
      From Aminet, util/libs/AmiSSL-v5-OS3.lha. Run its own installer;
      it needs an AmiSSL: assign, which the installer sets up.

  @{b}mathieeedoubbas.library and mathieeedoubtrans.library in LIBS:@{ub}
      Both ship with every Workbench install, so you almost certainly
      have them. AmiSSL's startup calls into them without checking, so
      if they are missing it does not report an error, it simply stops.
      Worth knowing if you are running a hand built boot disk.

@{" Contents " link Main}

@endnode


@node Install "Installation"

@{b}Installation@{ub}

1.  Install AmiSSL 5.x first, using its own installer.

2.  Copy the NextSync drawer wherever you keep your programs. There is
    nothing to configure by hand and nothing is written outside the
    drawer.

3.  Start NextSync. With no configuration it opens @{" Preferences " link Prefs}
    straight away.

The configuration is written to NextSync.conf in the same drawer as the
program, which is PROGDIR: as far as the program is concerned. Move the
drawer and the settings move with it.

To sync automatically at boot, put a line in S:User-Startup:

    @{b}NextSync SYNC >NIL:@{ub}

Note that this waits for the sync to finish before the rest of the
startup continues.

@{" Contents " link Main}

@endnode


@node Prefs "Setting it up"

@{b}Setting it up@{ub}

Preferences is under the Project menu, and opens on its own the first
time you run NextSync.

  @{b}Server@{ub}
      The host name of your Nextcloud, without https:// and without a
      path. For example cloud.example.com

  @{b}User@{ub}
      Your Nextcloud user name.

  @{b}Password@{ub}
      See below. The field shows asterisks; the character you have just
      typed stays legible until you type the next one, and is covered as
      soon as you leave the field.

  @{b}Port@{ub}
      443 for HTTPS, which is what you want. Anything else is treated as
      plain HTTP, which is only useful on a network you trust
      completely.

  @{b}Connect@{ub}
      Opens the connection with what you have typed. If it cannot, it
      says why and nothing is saved. If it can, the box below fills with
      the folders in your account.

  @{b}Folders@{ub}
      Click a folder to tick it. Ticked folders are the ones that get
      synced. Only the folders at the top level of your account are
      listed -- that is what you choose between; syncing then follows
      each one all the way down.

  @{b}Store in@{ub}
      The drawer the synced folders are kept in. Browse opens a file
      requester, or type a path. It is created if it does not exist. A
      folder called Documents ends up in <drawer>/Documents.

  @{b}Save@{ub}
      Writes NextSync.conf. Cancel leaves everything as it was.

@{b}About the password@{ub}

It is stored in plain text. There is no way around that: AmigaOS has
nowhere to keep a secret, and the password has to be presented to the
server on every single request, so it cannot be hashed either.

So use a Nextcloud @{b}app password@{ub} rather than your account password.
On the server, go to Settings, Security, and create one. It gets your
files and nothing else, you can revoke it on its own, and it will not
help anybody log in as you.

@{" Contents " link Main}

@endnode


@node Window "The window"

@{b}The window@{ub}

  @{b}Server@{ub}    the server and user currently configured

  @{b}Folders@{ub}   what will be synced, and where each one goes

  @{b}Status@{ub}    what is happening right now: the file being
            transferred and how far through the run it is

  @{b}Log@{ub}       one line per thing done, plus anything that went wrong

  @{b}Sync now@{ub}  start. While a sync runs the button reads @{b}Stop sync@{ub};
            pressing it, ESC, or the close gadget stops after the file
            being transferred finishes. Everything already transferred
            is kept, and the next run picks up from there.

  @{b}Quit@{ub}      leave. It will not quit in the middle of a sync; stop
            the sync first.

The menu holds About, Preferences, Snapshot -- which saves a picture of
the screen to out/ and is only there because it was useful while
building this -- and Quit.

@{" Contents " link Main}

@endnode


@node Shell "From the Shell"

@{b}From the Shell@{ub}

  @{b}NextSync@{ub}
      Opens the window.

  @{b}NextSync SYNC@{ub}
      Syncs everything and exits, printing what it does. This is the one
      for scripts and for S:User-Startup. CTRL-C stops it after the file
      being transferred finishes.

  @{b}NextSync LIST [dir]@{ub}
      Lists a directory on the server. With no argument, the top level.
      Useful for checking that a server and password work at all.

  @{b}NextSync PREFS@{ub}
      Opens Preferences on its own and exits when it closes.

Any of them can be followed by SNAPSHOT, which saves a picture of the
screen into out/ once the window has drawn itself and then exits.

@{b}Return codes@{ub}

   0   everything worked
   5   finished, but something failed or the run was stopped early
  10   could not connect, or the sync could not start
  20   no usable configuration

@{" Contents " link Main}

@endnode


@node Config "Configuration"

@{b}Configuration@{ub}

Preferences writes this for you, but it is a plain text file and you can
edit it if you would rather. It lives next to the program.

    # NextSync configuration
    server  cloud.example.com
    port    443
    user    yourname
    pass    your-app-password

    local   DH0:Nextcloud
    folder  Documents
    folder  Photos

  @{b}server@{ub}   host name only, no https:// and no path
  @{b}port@{ub}     443 means TLS; anything else is plain HTTP
  @{b}user@{ub}     your Nextcloud user name
  @{b}pass@{ub}     the password, which may contain spaces
  @{b}local@{ub}    the drawer the synced folders are kept in
  @{b}folder@{ub}   one line per folder to sync, up to 16

Lines starting with # or ; are ignored.

@{b}Pairs@{ub}

NextSync 1.0 had no local drawer and named both sides explicitly:

    pair    /Photos    Work:Cloud/Pictures

Those still work. They are synced as before, shown in Preferences, and
written back out untouched when you save -- so an old configuration keeps
working, and a folder that has to live somewhere unusual can still be
pinned by hand.

@{" Contents " link Main}

@endnode


@node Rules "How it decides"

@{b}How it decides what to do@{ub}

For every file, NextSync compares three things: what is on the server,
what is on disk, and what it recorded after the last successful sync. The
record is a file called .nextsync.state inside each synced drawer.

  server              local        last run   what happens
  ------------------  -----------  ---------  --------------------------
  there, etag changed unchanged    known      download
  unchanged           changed      known      upload
  there               gone         known      delete on the server
  gone                there        known      delete locally
  there               missing      unknown    download, it is new
  missing             there        unknown    upload, it is new
  changed             changed      known      conflict, see below

@{b}Conflicts@{ub}

If a file changed on both sides, NextSync does not choose. The local copy
is renamed to <name>.conflict and the server copy is downloaded, so you
have both and can sort it out yourself.

@{b}Deleting@{ub}

A deletion is only carried across for a file that the record shows was
synced before. So the first time you point NextSync at a drawer that
already has things in it, nothing is deleted anywhere -- everything that
exists on only one side is treated as new and copied to the other.

Delete .nextsync.state to make it forget and start over. Nothing is lost
by doing that; it just re-examines everything.

@{" Contents " link Main}

@endnode


@node Limits "Limits"

@{b}Limits@{ub}

  @{b}Names with a colon in them@{ub}
      Cannot exist on an Amiga filesystem, and are skipped with a line
      in the log. Length is left to the filesystem: FFS stops at 30
      characters, PFS3 and SFS allow about 100. A name that is too long
      shows up as one file that failed, not a failed sync.

  @{b}Non-ASCII names@{ub}
      Pass through as bytes. They look wrong in Workbench but they go
      back to the server unchanged, so nothing is lost.

  @{b}Times are UTC@{ub}
      On both sides. Set the Amiga clock to UTC, or accept a constant
      offset; the record settles after the first run either way.

  @{b}One thing at a time@{ub}
      One connection, one transfer. This is a 68020.

  @{b}Very large uploads@{ub}
      Nextcloud's chunked upload is not implemented, so a big upload
      depends on the server's request timeout.

  @{b}Files changed without changing@{ub}
      A file edited in place whose size and timestamp both stay the same
      is not noticed. Catching that means hashing every file on every
      run, which on this hardware costs more than it is worth.

@{" Contents " link Main}

@endnode


@node Trouble "Trouble"

@{b}When something goes wrong@{ub}

  @{b}Nothing happens at all, or the machine locks up on startup@{ub}
      Almost always the mathieee libraries. AmiSSL's startup opens
      mathieeedoubbas.library and mathieeedoubtrans.library and calls
      into them without checking whether they opened, so if they are
      missing from LIBS: it does not report anything, it simply stops.
      Both ship with Workbench.

  @{b}"connection failed"@{ub}
      The TCP/IP stack is not running, the name does not resolve, or
      nothing is listening. Try NextSync LIST from a Shell, which prints
      the reason. Check the server name has no https:// in front of it.

  @{b}Certificate errors@{ub}
      AmiSSL checks properly, which is the point. Make sure AmiSSL's
      certificate store is installed -- its installer does this -- and
      that the Amiga's clock is roughly right, since a certificate that
      is not valid yet fails just as hard as an expired one.

  @{b}"short transfer" or "connection lost mid-transfer"@{ub}
      The download did not arrive whole and was thrown away rather than
      kept. Nothing is broken; run it again.

  @{b}Out of memory@{ub}
      OpenSSL needs about 6 MB. Avail will tell you what you have.

  @{b}A file keeps failing@{ub}
      Look at its name. A colon cannot be stored, and FFS will not take
      a name over 30 characters.

@{" Contents " link Main}

@endnode


@node History "History"

@{b}History@{ub}

@{b}1.1@{ub}

  Preferences window: the server, the folders and the local drawer are
  all set up from the GUI. Connect tests the server and lists the
  folders on your account to choose from, and Browse picks the local
  drawer. It opens by itself on a first run.

  The password field no longer shows the password.

  A sync can be stopped while it runs, with the button, ESC, the close
  gadget, or CTRL-C from the Shell.

  Deleting a folder tree now clears the whole thing in one run, deepest
  drawer first, instead of leaving the empty drawers behind.

  A download that stops early is checked against the length the server
  declared, and a part file left behind by a crash is discarded rather
  than being mistaken for something of yours and uploaded.

@{b}1.0@{ub}

  First release. Two way sync, conflict handling, TLS through AmiSSL,
  Workbench and Shell interfaces.

@{" Contents " link Main}

@endnode


@node Author "Author"

@{b}Author@{ub}

Axel Hubinette
axel.hubinette@@gmail.com

@{b}Source@{ub}

    https://github.com/amiwoodcarver/nextsync-amiga

C, built with m68k-amigaos-gcc. The repository has the whole build
environment, the emulator test rig, and a file of notes on the AmigaOS
specifics that cost real time on the way -- what AmiSSL needs before it
will start, the amiga.lib sprintf that quietly shadows the real one, and
how you build a password gadget out of a string edit hook when GadTools
has no such thing.

Bug reports and patches are welcome.

@{b}Thanks@{ub}

To Jens Maus and everyone who works on AmiSSL. None of this would be
possible on a 68k Amiga in 2026 without it.

